{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Daniel, the pertinent information is: affective: perfectionism and asynchronous development; sensory: no primary concern; medical: none affecting school; and behaviour: may disengage when work lacks purpose. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: repetitive practice after mastery; fear of making visible mistakes; group tasks without meaningful challenge; and uneven executive skills on routine work. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Daniel, the most useful EA actions are to do not use more work as enrichment; normalize productive struggle; offer mentorship and flexible grouping; and teach planning for long inquiry projects. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-P-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.4625, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.475, "end": 1.75, "start_char": 22, "end_